Firm painless solitary neck nodule, Rapid nodular enlargement, Hoarseness/dysphagia, Cervical lymphadenopathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-044
Reason for Visit: Rapid enlargement of a neck lump, new-onset hoarseness, and sudden difficulty swallowing solid foods.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, rapid expansion of an anterior neck mass accompanied by new focal neurological signs (hoarseness indicating recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement) and mechanical compressive symptoms (dysphagia). Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to evaluate for impending airway compromise, acute intra-nodular hemorrhage, or rapidly progressive malignancy before standard imaging clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The lump in my neck has suddenly doubled in size this week, my voice has gone completely gravelly, and food is getting stuck in my throat."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a previously stable, painless, firm solitary nodule in the anterior neck present for six months. Over the past seven days, there has been a rapid enlargement of this thyroid nodule. Concurrently, the patient developed a sudden deepening of the voice and profound hoarseness. Over the last 48 hours, they have experienced progressive dysphagia, noting difficulty swallowing solid foods due to a sensation of oesophageal compression. Tonight, they complain of dyspnoea and a choking sensation when lying flat. They report a history of childhood head and neck irradiation for a benign condition decades ago.
Functional Status: Currently resting in a seated position; unable to sleep lying flat due to a choking sensation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute airway obstruction. High anxiety regarding the rapidly changing mass and potential malignancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 97 percent on room air | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Anxious, speaking with a distinct, newly hoarse voice, and leaning slightly forward.
Physical Exam: Head and neck exam reveals a 5cm, rock-hard, painless, solitary nodule in the right thyroid lobe. Crucially, the nodule exhibits fixation to the surrounding underlying strap muscles and does not move freely with swallowing. Palpable firm, non-tender cervical lymphadenopathy is present in the right anterior cervical chain. Oropharyngeal exam is clear. Respiratory exam reveals no overt stridor at rest, but mild dyspnea is provoked by lying supine. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Firm painless solitary neck nodule, Rapid nodular enlargement, Hoarseness/dysphagia, and Cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Extra pillows stacked on the bed confirm the patient's inability to sleep supine.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil relevant active medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected highly aggressive Thyroid Malignancy (e.g., Anaplastic or locally advanced papillary/medullary carcinoma) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Benign Multinodular Goitre - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a rock-hard, fixed, solitary nodule with accompanying cervical lymphadenopathy and acute recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy (hoarseness), which are overwhelming red flags for aggressive malignancy.

Acute Haemorrhage into a benign cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Haemorrhage typically presents with sudden, exquisitely localized pain. This nodule is distinctly painless despite its rapid, invasive expansion.

Subacute Thyroiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute lack of severe focal tenderness, fever, or preceding viral illness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a rapidly expanding, painless neck mass exhibiting overt signs of local tissue invasion (fixation, recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy causing hoarseness, and compressive dysphagia). Triage correctly escalated the dysphagia and dyspnea when lying flat as red flags for impending airway compromise. Objective evaluation confirmed a fixed, hard mass with regional lymphadenopathy, highly suspicious for an aggressive thyroid malignancy. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of acute tracheal occlusion during sleep. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to assess airway patency, secure an upright sleeping position, and coordinate urgent surgical and oncological evaluation, fully satisfying urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed the patient to strictly maintain a head-elevated position (at least 45 degrees) at all times to minimize tracheal compression and prevent positional asphyxia. Arranged an urgent, priority outpatient high-resolution neck ultrasound for tomorrow morning, with a direct referral to a tertiary head and neck surgeon for an urgent fine-needle aspiration (FNA) biopsy and airway assessment.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly sleeping propped up on a wedge pillow or recliner chair tonight (action), you utilize gravity to pull the neck mass slightly forward and away from your windpipe (cause), directly preventing the choking sensation and ensuring your airway remains open while you sleep (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Immediate tertiary surgical review required tomorrow following the ultrasound.

